  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
        "intro": "Angles use the **sexagesimal** system, based on sixties: $60$ seconds make a minute and $60$ minutes make a degree. Because a degree splits into $60$ parts rather than $10$, the halfway mark for rounding is $30$.",
        "facts": [
          "$1$ degree $= 60$ minutes, written $1^{\\circ}=60'$.",
          "$1$ minute $= 60$ seconds, written $1'=60''$.",
          "To change a decimal part of a degree into minutes, multiply it by $60$: $0.35^{\\circ}=0.35\\times 60=21'$.",
          "Rounding to the nearest degree makes the minutes the **critical unit**; rounding to the nearest minute makes the seconds the critical unit.",
          "**$30$ is the halfway mark**: round down if the critical unit is less than $30$, and up if it is $30$ or more.",
          "Rounding up from $59'$ gives $60'$, which is a whole degree: $46^{\\circ}\\,59'\\,38''\\approx 47^{\\circ}\\,0'$."
        ],
        "steps": [
          "Cut after the required unit",
          "Circle the critical unit right after the cut",
          "Round up if the critical unit is $30$ or more, down if it is less",
          "Write the rounded angle"
        ]
      },
